What the MCP Server Does

  MCP stands for Model Context Protocol - it's a tracking and monitoring server that runs alongside your privacy
  gap analysis workflow.

  Main Functions:

  1. Conversation Memory (Session Tracking)
  - Stores the entire conversation history for each analysis session
  - Logs all questions asked and answers received during the analysis
  - Allows you to retrieve previous analysis results by session ID

  2. Confidence Score Tracking
  - Records confidence scores for each gap identified in the analysis
  - Flags high-confidence findings (>80%) and low-confidence findings (<40%)
  - Helps you understand which gaps are certain vs. which need manual review

  3. Token Usage Monitoring
  - Tracks how many tokens (API usage) each analysis consumes
  - Records prompt tokens, completion tokens, and total tokens
  - Helps you monitor OpenAI API costs per analysis

  4. Gap Analysis Storage
  - Stores detailed results of each compliance gap analysis
  - Organizes gaps by company, priority level, and recommendations
  - Creates a searchable database of all analyses performed

  5. User Feedback Collection
  - Records thumbs up/down ratings on analysis quality
  - Tracks which questions/analyses were helpful vs. not helpful
  - Helps improve the system over time

  6. Debug & Monitoring Endpoints
  - /health - Check if server is running and view stats
  - /debug/gaps - View all gap analyses performed
  - /debug/tokens - See token usage across all analyses
  - /debug/memory - Inspect conversation history
  - /debug/confidence - Review confidence scores
  - /debug/feedback - See user feedback

  Why It's Optional But Recommended:

  Without MCP Server:
  - Analysis still works perfectly fine
  - You get the markdown and Word reports
  - But you lose tracking, history, and monitoring capabilities

  With MCP Server:
  - Full audit trail of all analyses performed
  - Token usage tracking for cost monitoring
  - Ability to review past analyses by session ID
  - Confidence scoring to identify uncertain findings
  - Better debugging when something goes wrong

  Real-World Analogy:

  Think of it like a flight data recorder (black box) for your privacy analysis system. The plane (analysis) flies
   without it, but you want it there to track what happened, monitor performance, and troubleshoot issues.

  In Practice:
  # Check server health
  curl http://localhost:8080/health

  # View all gap analyses performed
  curl http://localhost:8080/debug/gaps

  # See token usage stats
